NNAAMMEE
       lsipc - show information on IPC facilities currently employed in the
       system

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
       llssiippcc [options]

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       llssiippcc shows information on the System V inter-process communication
       facilities for which the calling process has read access.

OOPPTTIIOONNSS
       --ii, ----iidd _i_d
           Show full details on just the one resource element identified by
           _i_d. This option needs to be combined with one of the three resource
           options: --mm, --qq or --ss. It is possible to override the default
           output format for this option with the ----lliisstt, ----rraaww, ----jjssoonn or
           ----eexxppoorrtt option.

       --gg, ----gglloobbaall
           Show system-wide usage and limits of IPC resources. This option may
           be combined with one of the three resource options: --mm, --qq or --ss.
           The default is to show information about all resources.

       --hh, ----hheellpp
           Display help text and exit.

       --VV, ----vveerrssiioonn
           Print version and exit.

   RReessoouurrccee ooppttiioonnss
       --mm, ----sshhmmeemmss
           Write information about active shared memory segments.

       --qq, ----qquueeuueess
           Write information about active message queues.

       --ss, ----sseemmaapphhoorreess
           Write information about active semaphore sets.

   OOuuttppuutt ffoorrmmaattttiinngg
       --cc, ----ccrreeaattoorr
           Show creator and owner.

       --ee, ----eexxppoorrtt
           Produce output in the form of key="value" pairs. All potentially
           unsafe value characters are hex-escaped (\x<code>). See also option
           ----sshheellll.

       --JJ, ----jjssoonn
           Use the JSON output format.

       --ll, ----lliisstt
           Use the list output format. This is the default, except when ----iidd
           is used.

       --nn, ----nneewwlliinnee
           Display each piece of information on a separate line.

       ----nnoohheeaaddiinnggss
           Do not print a header line.

       ----nnoottrruunnccaattee
           Don’t truncate output.

       --oo, ----oouuttppuutt _l_i_s_t
           Specify which output columns to print. Use ----hheellpp to get a list of
           all supported columns.

       --bb, ----bbyytteess
           Print size in bytes rather than in human readable format.

       --rr, ----rraaww
           Raw output (no columnation).

       --tt, ----ttiimmee
           Write time information. The time of the last control operation that
           changed the access permissions for all facilities, the time of the
           last mmssggssnndd(2) and mmssggrrccvv(2) operations on message queues, the time
           of the last sshhmmaatt(2) and sshhmmddtt(2) operations on shared memory, and
           the time of the last sseemmoopp(2) operation on semaphores.

       ----ttiimmee--ffoorrmmaatt _t_y_p_e
           Display dates in short, full or iso format. The default is short,
           this time format is designed to be space efficient and human
           readable.

       --PP, ----nnuummeerriicc--ppeerrmmss
           Print numeric permissions in PERMS column.

       --yy, ----sshheellll
           The column name will be modified to contain only characters allowed
           for shell variable identifiers. This is usable, for example, with
           ----eexxppoorrtt. Note that this feature has been automatically enabled for
           ----eexxppoorrtt in version 2.37, but due to compatibility issues, now it’s
           necessary to request this behavior by ----sshheellll.

EEXXIITT SSTTAATTUUSS
       0
           if OK,

       1
           if incorrect arguments specified,

       2
           if a serious error occurs.

